package parser

// Package parser builds the syntax tree of one Tack source file. It is a
// port of the Go compiler's package parser, function by function, and
// accepts, rejects and recovers from exactly what Go's does: the same
// tree, the same errors in the same order, at the same places, with the
// same words. The grammar, the recovery and the limits are documented in
// full in Go's parser/doc.go; what follows is where the port differs and
// why.
//
// It sits between the lexer and the resolver. parse takes the lexer's
// Tokens, which end in an EOF token and already carry the inserted
// semicolons, and produces an ast.File: the package clause's name, the
// imports, every top-level declaration in source order, and every
// comment, and the syntax errors it recovered from. Go's NewParser,
// Limit and Parse are the one function parse here.
//
// Comment tokens are set apart before parsing, so the grammar never sees
// them. The parser is recursive descent, as Go's is, but for binary
// operators: Go climbs their precedences by recursion, a frame for each
// precedence an expression steps through, and parse_expr reduces them
// with stacks of the parser's own instead, building the same tree.
//
// Stack. The parser recurses on the C stack, which does not grow, to the
// nesting limit, so what each level costs is kept small: the parser's
// recursive functions do not call Parser methods that would copy the
// Parser, a large value, onto their stacks (see look.tck), nor keep a
// node's Kind copied while they recurse (see nodes.tck). A program nests at most max_nesting
// levels deep, a thousand, counted together across expressions,
// statements and types (see nest); the first construct past it is
// reported once, parsing stops, and the file has no tree.
//
// Failures. Go's constructs return their errors as a []diag.Diagnostic,
// which holds one at most. Here a construct returns a Fail, a small
// copyable value naming its one error among the parser's pending ones,
// whose messages are kept in one list: an error made and then dropped, as
// recovery drops some, costs a few bytes and no Diagnostic. record keeps
// the ones recovery keeps, and parse makes Diagnostics of those at the
// end, in the order they were recorded. Every function that can recurse
// returns an error, the builtin append's out of memory, beside its
// result.
//
// Nodes. A construct builds its node's payload as a local value, Go's
// struct, and adds it to the tree once it parsed, so a construct that
// fails leaves nodes no one names, which the tree keeps and nothing
// reads. Where Go sets a field of a node already made, through a
// pointer, the port copies the node's kind out, changes it and writes it
// back. Go's map from a parenthesized expression to its parentheses'
// span is a map from the node's id. Go's maps of import names and of the
// type parameters in scope are lists searched in order: a file has few.
//
// Tokens. Go's peek and next return nil past the final EOF token, which
// no path that does not panic in Go reads; the port's return the EOF
// token there. Go counts the tokens a parse looks at, for its tests that
// bound the work; the port does not.
//
// Recovery's look aheads that take a function in Go, a stop condition or
// a token match, take a flag saying which of Go's functions it is here,
// since Tack has no closures but scope parameters.
//
// Builtin mode, in which the file documenting the builtins parses, is
// ported but never on: the loader parses every file with it off, and the
// builtins come later in the port.
